- recheck: verify-1 §2; or simulate the recurrence and measure $(\hat p_{t+1} - \hat p_t)/(y_t - \hat p_t)$ over the last 500 of 4000 steps at $\lambda \in \{0, 0.1, 0.5, 0.9, 0.99\}$

Under the recurrence $\alpha \mapsto \lambda\alpha + y$, $\beta \mapsto \lambda\beta + (1-y)$, the gain on one step is $1/(\lambda n + 1) = 1/n'$, the post-update count. At the stationary count $n_{\text{eff}} = 1/(1-\lambda)$ this is exactly $1-\lambda$, not $(1-\lambda)/(2-\lambda)$; its supremum is $1$ at $\lambda = 0$, where $\hat p = y$. Prop C.1 substituted $n_{\text{eff}}$ into B.1's $1/(n+1)$, a count the update never adds to. Measured gains matched $1-\lambda$ to six decimals at every $\lambda$ tested. The ceiling $\rho_\Sigma \lt R_\Sigma/2$ falls; the single-edge reachable region is $\rho_\Sigma \lt R_\Sigma$.

